Similar running shoes

Saucony Ride 19 vs. Saucony Triumph 24

Price: $170Weight (per shoe): 8.8 oz men's; 7.6 oz women'sHeel-to-toe drop: 10 mmStack height: 43 - 33 mmCushion level: MaximumSupport type: Neutral

The Saucony Triumph 24 is a plush daily trainer that offers more cushion than the Saucony Ride 19, and is thus better suited for recovery runs, easier runs, and folks who want a little more cushion underfoot for their joints.

Choose the Triumph if you want more cushion, especially for daily training and recovery runs. Choose the Ride 19 if you want a more do-it-all daily trainer that can dip into faster workout speeds with more ease. Learn more in our in-depth Saucony Triumph 24 review.

Saucony Ride 19 vs. HOKA Mach 7

Price: $145Weight (per shoe): 8.4 oz men's; 7.2 oz women'sHeel-to-toe drop: 5 mmStack height: 36.8 - 31.8 mm (men's), 35.6 - 30.6 mm (women's)Cushion level: ModerateSupport type: Neutral

Like the Saucony Ride 19, the HOKA Mach 7 is a do-it-all daily trainer at the same price. The Mach 7 is lighter and snappier, though, so it transitions better into workouts. The Saucony Ride, on the other hand, will serve you better for the easier days when you might want more cushion and support underfoot. The Mach 7 was the top pick in our women’s running shoes guide. Learn more in our in-depth review of the HOKA Mach 7 road running shoes.

Saucony Ride 19 vs. Brooks Glycerin Max 2

Price: $200Weight (per shoe): 11.3 oz men's; 10.2 oz women'sHeel-to-toe drop: 6 mmStack height: 45 - 39 mmCushion level: MaximumSupport type: Neutral

The Brooks Glycerin Max 2 is, in some ways, the antithesis of the Saucony Ride 19 because it has a lot of cushion, a lot of stack (45-39 mm), an aggressive GlideRoll rocker to encourage forward movement, and is much more expensive. This is a daily trainer designed for runners who want to feel fast, even on regular mileage days, and who want a daily trainer that will encourage faster turnover on long runs or workout days while still providing a lot of cushion.

The Glycerin Max 2 is among the most maximalist shoes we’ve tested, and it’s at the other end of the spectrum from barefoot shoes and zero-drop road running shoes, like the Altra Torin. If you want more cushion and turnover than the Saucony Ride 19offerss, and you have the budget, this is a very popular shoe.

FAQ

Does the Saucony Ride 19 fit true to size?

Yes, in our testing. It comes in regular and wide, and both our narrow-to-regular and average-to-wide testers were comfortable in the regular fit.

What changed from the Ride 18?

With the Ride 19 you get a reformulated PWRRUN+ midsole with slightly more cushion and responsiveness, a stretchier engineered-mesh upper, and 1 mm more stack. We only noticed the differences side by side.

Can you run trails in the Ride 19?

The Ride 19 handled gravel, dirt roads and non-technical trails fine, but it isn't a trail shoe.

Why you should trust us / About the author

Becca Downs ran D1 cross country and track for Indiana University–Purdue University Indianapolis (IUPUI) and continued racing everything from elite miles to an ultramarathon in her post-collegiate years. She earned a USATF Level 1 certification and coached boys and girls high school cross country and track in Indianapolis for four years. Becca then joined the IUPUI women’s distance coaching staff as a volunteer assistant coach in 2020-2021. She also notched a few small FKTs under her belt.

She geeks out on trail running, track and field, and cries every time she watches a marathon (they’re just so beautiful!). She earned her MFA in Creative Writing from the Mile-High MFA Program at Regis University. Becca is based in Denver, Colorado.

